From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PixelOS PIF <pif@localhost>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:32:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] key attestation: port TrickyStore keybox support

Replace the inherited keybox implementation with a TrickyStore-style port so
Play Integrity reports basic, device and strong integrity.

The inherited code had three defects that each independently block a verdict:

  - The attested identity tags (710/711/712/716/717) were never rewritten. GMS
    requests attestation with the handset's own ODM properties while the PIF
    hook replaces the process Build fields, so the certificate and the Build
    disagreed. BASIC does not cross-check the two; DEVICE and STRONG do.
  - The keybox was parsed into flat EC./RSA. maps ordered by string key, which
    broke chains longer than three certificates, and the bundled Settings
    importer rejected single-algorithm documents. RKP keyboxes carry one
    algorithm and are the ones that reach a STRONG verdict.
  - No ECDSA JCA service exists in the platform BouncyCastle (Android uses
    Conscrypt for that), so any path using Signature.getInstance(..., "BC") or
    JcaContentSignerBuilder("SHA256withECDSA") fails on device. Leaf generation
    now signs and verifies through the BC light API.

New code:

  android.security.trickystore.AttestationExtensionBuilder
      Pure KeyMint KeyDescription encoder, ascending tag order as real KeyMint
      emits it, so the exact bytes are covered by host tests.

  android.security.trickystore.AttestationLeafRewriter
      Re-signs a hardware attestation leaf with the keybox key. Preserves tag
      704 (RootOfTrust) byte for byte so the verified-boot measurement stays
      genuine, rewrites the identity and patch levels from the profile, and
      drops serial/IMEI/MEID/second-IMEI. Re-signing an already signed leaf is a
      no-op so certificate bytes stay stable between calls.

  android.security.trickystore.LeafCertificateBuilder / CertificateGenerator
      Software attestation chain for devices whose TEE cannot attest at all
      (the ColorOS -49 case in the reference write-up).

  android.security.trickystore.BcCrypto
      Shared X.509 signing and verification on the BC light API. The digest
      follows the algorithm name (SHA-1/224/256/384/512): the platform has no
      ECDSA JCA service, so this is the only signing path, and a fixed SHA-256
      would produce a certificate whose SignatureAlgorithm OID disagrees with
      the bytes actually signed whenever a keybox key or attested digest is
      SHA-384 or SHA-512.

  android.security.trickystore.KeyBoxManager
      RKP and legacy XML parsing with private-key/certificate match and
      issuer-signature chain validation. A truncated or keyless document keeps
      the previously loaded keybox instead of clearing it.

  android.security.trickystore.TrickyStoreService
      Keybox, target list and patch levels from Settings.Secure; per-package
      AUTO/LEAF_HACK/GENERATE/SKIP policy; five-minute TEE probe cache so one
      transient Keystore failure does not force software generation until reboot.

  android.security.pif.PlayIntegrityProfile
      Read-only view of the profile Play Integrity Fix already applies, using the
      same PIF_DATA / FETCHED_PIF / config_certifiedBuildProperties precedence and
      the same fingerprint derivation PIF itself uses.

Attestation is only rewritten for callers the PIF hook itself spoofs
(com.google.android.gms and com.android.vending), and only for attested RSA/EC
keys, so unrelated applications keep their genuine hardware attestation.

Also fixes profile application in PropImitationHooks: the certified props list
was appended to on every GMS restart instead of being rebuilt, Build.VERSION
members were looked up on Build and silently dropped, and long/boolean fields
were written with putObject.

Host tests cover the extension encoder, keybox parsing (including the RKP
single-algorithm shape, the rejection cases and reload behaviour), hardware leaf
re-signing and software chain generation, using real ECDSA/RSA material.

Base: 68f9156be335
